  'Introduction to Religious Studies',
  'Introduction to Religious Studies',
  'Introduction to Religious Studies introduces students to the academic study of religion as a complex human phenomenon expressed through texts, rituals, bodies, spaces, ethics, institutions, politics, and everyday life. Students learn how scholars define religion, compare traditions responsibly, interpret primary sources, analyze lived practice, and discuss religious diversity without reducing traditions to stereotypes or devotional claims.',
  $irelsjson${"slug": "introduction-to-religious-studies", "title": "Introduction to Religious Studies", "description": "Introduction to Religious Studies introduces students to the academic study of religion as a complex human phenomenon expressed through texts, rituals, bodies, spaces, ethics, institutions, politics, and everyday life. Students learn how scholars define religion, compare traditions responsibly, interpret primary sources, analyze lived practice, and discuss religious diversity without reducing traditions to stereotypes or devotional claims.", "topics": [{"id": "6ea152ad-9b82-4f18-9b90-23c3246e18e8", "type": "topic", "title": "Religion as an Academic Object of Study", "description": "This opening section establishes the course's non-devotional, non-sectarian frame. Students learn to study religion as a human, historical, social, textual, material, and experiential phenomenon while taking practitioners seriously.", "children": [{"id": "df2a9c1a-dcab-4b82-ad47-18af0dfc6d72", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Academic vs. confessional study", "children": [{"id": "9612ff0f-f57e-4ffd-a606-6b13791bf3f6", "type": "concept", "title": "Theology, apologetics, and religious studies"}, {"id": "52d569a5-5e1c-4b35-bb16-95d33c84691f", "type": "concept", "title": "Insider and outsider perspectives"}, {"id": "2fbef32e-054f-4b49-802c-1d014b9ba0c9", "type": "concept", "title": "Description before evaluation"}], "description": "Students learn the difference between studying religion from within a tradition and analyzing religion with public, evidence-based academic tools."}, {"id": "f5e1fe7e-a413-4430-87e5-1ea120f548c9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Religion in human life", "children": [{"id": "cfe4c778-76b6-4e42-a9e5-cae0e37205e1", "type": "concept", "title": "Belief and practice"}, {"id": "84dbc32d-abe0-4fe0-97e0-5a07fbf03268", "type": "concept", "title": "Identity and belonging"}, {"id": "7fa581bb-e983-4fda-8a34-56a1917317e2", "type": "concept", "title": "Public and private religion"}], "description": "The goal is to see religion as a force in meaning-making, identity, institutions, art, law, politics, conflict, and care."}, {"id": "adfa65a6-1228-496f-90ff-62353cc2644f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Religious literacy", "children": [{"id": "d872f951-663c-4bc1-a591-09e09c5dd961", "type": "concept", "title": "Basic vocabulary"}, {"id": "202eedbf-40ae-4075-a621-322cb7725815", "type": "concept", "title": "Avoiding essentialism"}, {"id": "79cb4fab-c598-42e7-943e-c7b54792b9c2", "type": "concept", "title": "Public consequences of religious ignorance"}], "description": "Students practice accurate, respectful, and critical explanation of traditions without stereotyping or flattening difference."}]}, {"id": "c2cdc9fa-73bd-48d8-a9df-4330da3560ec", "type": "topic", "title": "Defining Religion: The Sacred, Lived Religion, and Critical Categories", "description": "Definitions of religion are tools, not neutral facts. This section helps students understand why definitions matter, what they reveal, and what they obscure.", "children": [{"id": "1818a6e5-5fe5-485e-9a9a-b81245e4f515", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Substantive and functional definitions", "children": [{"id": "834f4eac-d579-4d2e-9920-a85bd1e02604", "type": "concept", "title": "Sacred/profane"}, {"id": "5046cd6a-9553-4bf5-87ab-f25df7e828cf", "type": "concept", "title": "Ultimate concern"}, {"id": "dcc5be8d-0511-44ff-b4e4-6094e7ef1a62", "type": "concept", "title": "Social function"}], "description": "Students compare definitions that focus on gods, sacred realities, ultimate concern, social cohesion, experience, or practice."}, {"id": "9cd07bd0-444d-4440-8df3-8bb26baed34c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Lived religion", "children": [{"id": "59075ddd-b664-423b-b169-fcbef9ae4291", "type": "concept", "title": "Everyday practice"}, {"id": "36964ca6-21b2-46e1-bc3e-46691380e67b", "type": "concept", "title": "Domestic religion"}, {"id": "de1a0c2d-8801-4e93-bc02-a2e783b7e651", "type": "concept", "title": "Popular religion"}], "description": "This approach shifts attention from official doctrine to what people actually do in homes, streets, bodies, calendars, and online spaces."}, {"id": "590f9e0d-4ee2-4f6b-817e-4530d5af2c29", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Category critique", "children": [{"id": "b97f615d-a958-4abd-8579-a7e80909a04d", "type": "concept", "title": "World religions model"}, {"id": "5f60d820-5613-4601-84be-620c408b83ce", "type": "concept", "title": "Race and empire"}, {"id": "12860c53-6889-4123-8e75-7aa3db3d333a", "type": "concept", "title": "Religion vs. culture"}], "description": "Students ask who invented categories, who benefits from them, and how colonial, legal, or academic classification shapes what counts as religion."}]}, {"id": "3c5f0270-5ced-423e-bfca-c97a98079426", "type": "topic", "title": "Methods and Evidence: Text, History, Ethnography, Material Culture, and Digital Sources", "description": "An introductory course teaches students how knowledge is made. This section introduces the major evidence types and methods used by scholars of religion.", "children": [{"id": "0b2004a7-5083-47d0-90d6-2be10fd922f5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Textual and historical methods", "children": [{"id": "f03eeb00-7d0e-4489-9a3a-a990b46ea89b", "type": "concept", "title": "Authorship and audience"}, {"id": "2666c3e2-2a32-4e21-887a-a93ce20c1c7c", "type": "concept", "title": "Canon and commentary"}, {"id": "29778195-7185-46c6-9105-1066f5d037a8", "type": "concept", "title": "Historical-critical questions"}], "description": "Students learn to read sources in context, identify genre, track transmission, and distinguish primary from secondary evidence."}, {"id": "a030d526-1773-4848-88d4-2f986060292b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ethnography and field observation", "children": [{"id": "7a47bdfd-cbc1-4585-ad37-4a6bc072587d", "type": "concept", "title": "Participant observation"}, {"id": "0d8c691c-27de-4239-ab3c-050163179aa4", "type": "concept", "title": "Interview ethics"}, {"id": "8c7d4176-83e6-4c81-86c9-98a4acfb6cd9", "type": "concept", "title": "Thick description"}], "description": "Students learn how interviews, observation, fieldnotes, and reflexivity help scholars study living communities responsibly."}, {"id": "789424ab-e2f4-45e7-9c1a-fd180cc2cd2e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Material and digital religion", "children": [{"id": "9c6959aa-9560-4c4e-85ff-3f969811846c", "type": "concept", "title": "Sacred space"}, {"id": "75447d4c-2841-4212-b85c-6bb070335a7d", "type": "concept", "title": "Digital rituals"}, {"id": "f3471345-b1f2-4880-abfd-edd45379d58c", "type": "concept", "title": "Archives and metadata"}], "description": "Students treat objects, architecture, sound, images, apps, and online communities as evidence rather than decoration."}]}, {"id": "bffd6340-832c-4d82-9d5a-c1f0a61714a6", "type": "topic", "title": "Myth, Narrative, Scripture, and Interpretation", "description": "Stories and texts do religious work: they organize memory, authority, ethics, identity, and time. This section trains students to interpret them carefully.", "children": [{"id": "1fb86d70-a485-4aea-b91b-a9b6a0357479", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Myth and sacred narrative", "children": [{"id": "bf51d643-cff3-425f-842f-918bf0eb1972", "type": "concept", "title": "Origins and cosmogony"}, {"id": "dc0a52b9-ee39-4e9c-b17c-fecce9885ed1", "type": "concept", "title": "Heroes and ancestors"}, {"id": "4c26a4c8-1fe5-42d5-be16-ba8ecc983259", "type": "concept", "title": "Ritual retelling"}], "description": "Students learn that myth is not simply falsehood; in religious studies it often names meaningful narrative that structures a world."}, {"id": "1e9c949e-ccf8-4934-ba83-5b46763c1b9d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Scripture and canon", "children": [{"id": "39a67341-b538-4d21-a50f-0bef4b1a584b", "type": "concept", "title": "Canon formation"}, {"id": "908eb686-b0a8-41ed-ac10-3fa6361c682c", "type": "concept", "title": "Translation"}, {"id": "3d7f43ef-a829-4993-9f52-5809a4729b2f", "type": "concept", "title": "Commentary traditions"}], "description": "Students explore how communities authorize texts, preserve them, translate them, and debate their meanings."}, {"id": "157fb6a0-5f6e-4146-ae98-929572b8a0fc", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Hermeneutics", "children": [{"id": "ff231fc8-bfa5-4c12-b18d-2569492ad5c1", "type": "concept", "title": "Reader and community"}, {"id": "2e529813-c660-4515-90fc-2504cfd3bc34", "type": "concept", "title": "Context and reception"}, {"id": "f08d8e79-eaa8-447e-8db2-ac356a9c4a03", "type": "concept", "title": "Interpretive conflict"}], "description": "Students compare interpretive strategies across literal, allegorical, legal, mystical, historical, and liberationist readings."}]}, {"id": "717cc052-0ce9-4598-8e51-8064505e2fdf", "type": "topic", "title": "Ritual, Practice, Embodiment, and Religious Experience", "description": "Religion is not only believed; it is enacted. This section helps students analyze repeated practices, disciplined bodies, emotion, memory, and experience.", "children": [{"id": "f3dc693f-5efd-4cca-b683-affe5c852d15", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ritual form and function", "children": [{"id": "fad2cade-8345-4314-85c9-a5623b8a7ef3", "type": "concept", "title": "Rites of passage"}, {"id": "68d36b62-5e26-4be0-9759-30024bc9316f", "type": "concept", "title": "Calendar rituals"}, {"id": "b7b3777d-ab2a-462d-afcd-d76c53add652", "type": "concept", "title": "Purity and sacrifice"}], "description": "Students learn to identify ritual sequences, roles, objects, spaces, and social effects."}, {"id": "50c2ab8b-ae2f-469c-9a9a-ac8679d43d00", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Embodiment and discipline", "children": [{"id": "69f85165-4666-48a2-8128-d73135d7bf8a", "type": "concept", "title": "Asceticism"}, {"id": "46693568-407e-4a1d-a47c-d8b4f33b99d1", "type": "concept", "title": "Sensory practice"}, {"id": "3e2f5a51-c175-4ba2-83eb-c9fb1297109d", "type": "concept", "title": "Gendered bodies"}], "description": "The section highlights bodies in prayer, fasting, meditation, pilgrimage, dress, food, healing, and performance."}, {"id": "8f50b07d-a426-4648-a975-f9d7068333e2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Religious experience", "children": [{"id": "2952f7a3-a6cb-4f3a-97bf-084887e8745d", "type": "concept", "title": "Mysticism"}, {"id": "12f85307-3121-4ff5-b703-531cf7cf0af9", "type": "concept", "title": "Conversion narratives"}, {"id": "c1d03d81-c43d-42e6-b7d1-207296c47f51", "type": "concept", "title": "Psychology and culture"}], "description": "Students consider visions, conversion, mystical states, possession, awe, grief, and healing without reducing them to one explanation."}]}, {"id": "ff783fdb-9241-4fd6-b0c6-7d02315bb183", "type": "topic", "title": "Symbol, Image, Sound, Space, and Material Culture", "description": "Material culture shows how religion becomes visible, audible, tangible, and contested. Students learn to analyze things and spaces as arguments about value and authority.", "children": [{"id": "1835dafa-baee-4205-b22d-a64af4f8113a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Symbols and images", "children": [{"id": "9e12e7ba-0df4-4c37-bd5e-e6d602bad06f", "type": "concept", "title": "Icon and iconoclasm"}, {"id": "1a7d977b-e689-49c4-97da-5b3a2f1f8fb1", "type": "concept", "title": "Dress and identity"}, {"id": "33ceba4a-2b0b-4df7-942a-aca1a1e76dbf", "type": "concept", "title": "Art and devotion"}], "description": "Students ask how signs, icons, clothing, colors, and visual forms condense complex meanings."}, {"id": "26e3e1a6-6732-4d50-ad49-d0171dd51922", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Sacred space", "children": [{"id": "5d5ba2bf-297a-4650-a90c-88b85ef57d32", "type": "concept", "title": "Architecture"}, {"id": "bdbb82d8-a372-40c9-9344-c6503228f0d8", "type": "concept", "title": "Pilgrimage sites"}, {"id": "b0e5c620-33e9-4973-8879-8e361ce51657", "type": "concept", "title": "Place-making"}], "description": "Students analyze temples, churches, mosques, synagogues, shrines, homes, landscapes, memorials, and digital spaces."}, {"id": "6c917a16-cc58-4b8a-808c-500f4f27d0a0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Sound, food, and objects", "children": [{"id": "f0feac86-5ecb-41a9-a769-decb90dfd8f7", "type": "concept", "title": "Music and chant"}, {"id": "54fd6bf8-e2e5-492d-ba64-cb775747be92", "type": "concept", "title": "Food laws"}, {"id": "53c887b8-9f51-4b16-802b-a7194a65e4fb", "type": "concept", "title": "Relics and commodities"}], "description": "The goal is to notice how music, chant, bells, foodways, relics, tools, and commodities carry religious meaning."}]}, {"id": "fadfc15e-b9a7-4c7c-b90a-ebf033e8c4de", "type": "topic", "title": "Community, Authority, Institutions, and Tradition-Making", "description": "Traditions are maintained and changed by communities. This section studies leadership, law, lineage, institutions, memory, reform, and conflict.", "children": [{"id": "05034e21-eae1-427c-8ac2-cafadd9ef564", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Authority and leadership", "children": [{"id": "b839de5c-c934-440b-a18f-71191daa767c", "type": "concept", "title": "Priests, rabbis, imams, monks, gurus"}, {"id": "8c02123b-5b23-4156-9bb4-e4809b19f493", "type": "concept", "title": "Charisma and office"}, {"id": "a439a876-e349-4ba1-a82f-fce2b67797bb", "type": "concept", "title": "Gender and authority"}], "description": "Students examine how authority is justified through charisma, office, scripture, lineage, expertise, revelation, or community recognition."}, {"id": "4b9c39d0-6a12-4fb6-a39a-34d79db9d23c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Institutions and law", "children": [{"id": "065cc13f-d312-4e57-aaed-091fd1f0aefd", "type": "concept", "title": "Congregations and orders"}, {"id": "acffe68e-d7e5-4c47-97b5-2d69ea51b2c5", "type": "concept", "title": "Religious law"}, {"id": "b0ee5291-4a91-4b5f-822d-862086c8fdc4", "type": "concept", "title": "Institutional accountability"}], "description": "Students learn how religious organizations structure education, charity, discipline, courts, finance, and public influence."}, {"id": "cd68e5c9-f0b0-48d5-bb7f-4ece4cea3eb2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Continuity and change", "children": [{"id": "6a8f99c1-81cb-4711-a347-f2c00ceaf0cb", "type": "concept", "title": "Reform movements"}, {"id": "0217a951-7038-4f57-8462-d051e8f835a4", "type": "concept", "title": "Sect and denomination"}, {"id": "b2635ebb-cc7e-4d8a-9a4a-cc09d469d5a3", "type": "concept", "title": "Memory and invention"}], "description": "The section frames reform, revival, schism, syncretism, and innovation as normal parts of religious history."}]}, {"id": "cc3bd060-669d-43ea-b856-58a1ef52f2d2", "type": "topic", "title": "Comparison, Translation, Classification, and World Religions", "description": "Comparison is powerful but risky. Students learn to compare responsibly, recognize the limits of the world religions model, and avoid false equivalence.", "children": [{"id": "a06a9bdf-c7cc-43e9-915e-0056397d3e8f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Why compare?", "children": [{"id": "f79db83c-364c-4e9e-a1e8-dadc6639fc77", "type": "concept", "title": "Controlled comparison"}, {"id": "0a5bd0a2-9be6-4bad-ae45-ea5261043e9b", "type": "concept", "title": "Difference and similarity"}, {"id": "f879deac-ea06-42e2-9612-044fb6a44b83", "type": "concept", "title": "Scale of analysis"}], "description": "Students learn how comparison can clarify patterns, differences, questions, and assumptions when done with precision."}, {"id": "76209bdf-4688-41ba-9cef-eea257091a9f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Translation problems", "children": [{"id": "d5294f87-f7a6-4fe8-8414-a61e0b89f5fa", "type": "concept", "title": "Untranslatable terms"}, {"id": "38a9bd38-110d-48be-8171-956f5c7a07b3", "type": "concept", "title": "Colonial translation"}, {"id": "f87acc64-6f7d-4daf-9286-342b0a2fd21c", "type": "concept", "title": "Conceptual humility"}], "description": "Students explore why terms such as religion, scripture, god, ritual, caste, dharma, and secular do not map cleanly across languages."}, {"id": "38931215-39d8-463b-bdcc-9349cbbb68af", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Classification and power", "children": [{"id": "dbe5febf-d2c3-4e65-8efc-4c527dcac80b", "type": "concept", "title": "World religions textbooks"}, {"id": "862ff9d0-d566-4cf6-995b-c8e0f267a068", "type": "concept", "title": "Legal definitions"}, {"id": "a8afd787-4b44-4d99-bf1f-5f2b3c12d87d", "type": "concept", "title": "Minority traditions"}], "description": "The section asks how museum labels, census categories, law, textbooks, and media shape public understanding."}]}, {"id": "105b29dd-293f-408b-8001-c1abd045bf2d", "type": "topic", "title": "Indigenous, Diasporic, and Local Traditions", "description": "This section resists treating religion as only formal doctrine or large world traditions. It centers place, land, memory, kinship, diaspora, and colonial history.", "children": [{"id": "e397c500-9e30-4ea3-a1b6-9ce4d408e646", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Indigenous traditions and land", "children": [{"id": "d864d6aa-b3ba-4640-b488-7fd6316ab4f1", "type": "concept", "title": "Oral tradition"}, {"id": "6de7d96b-159f-48cf-b394-e2cbe1c10a1c", "type": "concept", "title": "Sacred landscapes"}, {"id": "8f34bb33-e597-45d2-8afe-763665ff2d09", "type": "concept", "title": "Colonial disruption"}], "description": "Students learn why land, ancestors, ceremony, ecology, and sovereignty are often inseparable."}, {"id": "be50cc96-c736-4e84-b43e-defec740179b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Diaspora and migration", "children": [{"id": "e95d49f9-dd32-4e86-944e-ba2d94a81739", "type": "concept", "title": "Temples and community centers"}, {"id": "03c83ded-4da5-44af-af0d-e66d0b3dd634", "type": "concept", "title": "Hybridity"}, {"id": "5125c868-9f29-450a-be5c-83c35b703ad7", "type": "concept", "title": "Generational change"}], "description": "Students study how religious communities adapt across borders while preserving memory, ritual, and identity."}, {"id": "2b50ac68-c576-48e9-984d-54570e654300", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Local religion", "children": [{"id": "38693969-cc62-4e2c-870f-878b99f85ab3", "type": "concept", "title": "Household shrines"}, {"id": "1a171277-9004-45dd-9c91-cba9dcbe28f9", "type": "concept", "title": "Festivals"}, {"id": "c8c79b6f-252c-4760-8947-14014606a7fa", "type": "concept", "title": "Neighborhood religious economies"}], "description": "The goal is to see local practice as theoretically important, not a deviation from official tradition."}]}, {"id": "c0a1ead9-ef46-4e20-a5fd-433e33a8e0a5", "type": "topic", "title": "Asian Religious Traditions in Context", "description": "This section introduces major Asian traditions through history, practice, texts, philosophy, and lived communities while avoiding one-note summaries.", "children": [{"id": "c8f840f2-87c1-4aed-b6f9-996936a23651", "type": "subtopic", "title": "South Asian traditions", "children": [{"id": "2ab69dad-f0d7-4286-b970-a78189140903", "type": "concept", "title": "Vedas and Upanishads"}, {"id": "67e10794-f98d-4809-91bc-216a88e5daec", "type": "concept", "title": "Bhakti and puja"}, {"id": "8497ffb8-b5af-4e6b-9aa0-c1b3a597515c", "type": "concept", "title": "Karma, dharma, moksha"}], "description": "Students encounter Hindu, Buddhist, Jain, and Sikh traditions through ritual, philosophy, devotion, caste, renunciation, and modernity."}, {"id": "e607cd11-aea6-44d4-acee-0851bd675727", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Buddhist traditions", "children": [{"id": "9b3fc021-085c-48fb-8e02-b8e0ed375209", "type": "concept", "title": "Four Noble Truths"}, {"id": "c5ee17a1-a1f3-4cc6-9094-98baa292da37", "type": "concept", "title": "Sangha"}, {"id": "fb0e335c-4da1-43eb-9c6d-a9915889a183", "type": "concept", "title": "Bodhisattva ideals"}], "description": "Students trace Buddhism from early teachings to Theravada, Mahayana, Vajrayana, monasticism, meditation, and global modern forms."}, {"id": "48daa5fa-cbcc-40ce-84b5-6001c225809e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "East Asian traditions", "children": [{"id": "dafba335-a27c-49eb-be18-790f38c3d6bd", "type": "concept", "title": "Ancestor rites"}, {"id": "18f311fa-8321-49f0-9413-20ab2ac71280", "type": "concept", "title": "Dao and ritual"}, {"id": "197d5c1c-0d0f-4d37-882c-3b49511a5921", "type": "concept", "title": "Kami and shrine practice"}], "description": "Students examine Confucian, Daoist, Buddhist, Shinto, and popular traditions in relation to family, state, ritual, and cosmology."}]}, {"id": "03e77945-c930-46cd-be0d-88d884e2de3b", "type": "topic", "title": "Abrahamic Traditions in Context", "description": "Students study Judaism, Christianity, and Islam historically and comparatively without treating them as identical branches of a single template.", "children": [{"id": "a5a36669-1c1f-4858-9e4e-8be8e7ccd418", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Judaism", "children": [{"id": "950d7a66-d2d5-4cbd-916d-78f5de6206e4", "type": "concept", "title": "Tanakh and Talmud"}, {"id": "f2e3a722-0370-4dd7-968b-c17e2d6d7975", "type": "concept", "title": "Halakhah"}, {"id": "94e1033d-44ca-44d8-9d73-1fb51249175f", "type": "concept", "title": "Diaspora and modern Jewish life"}], "description": "Students explore Torah, rabbinic tradition, law, ritual calendar, diaspora, memory, modernity, and diverse Jewish identities."}, {"id": "1f0d2a16-74a2-4c22-83f8-49653a17d003", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Christianity", "children": [{"id": "59a04f42-b854-4467-a7c3-ffb5f86221dc", "type": "concept", "title": "New Testament contexts"}, {"id": "81868e74-fd86-440d-b4f3-59ae54817e72", "type": "concept", "title": "Creeds and councils"}, {"id": "d4e640a9-ac69-4da9-87cf-3f7614105291", "type": "concept", "title": "Reformation and global Christianity"}], "description": "Students study Jesus movements, church formation, doctrine, sacraments, reform, missions, colonialism, and global Christianity."}, {"id": "ecbb325d-a057-40d4-980b-a38c7d21005d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Islam", "children": [{"id": "e1464c44-320d-494d-9455-09e2b474144f", "type": "concept", "title": "Quran and hadith"}, {"id": "0914add5-c1aa-4784-95b9-d9137d26a5c1", "type": "concept", "title": "Sharia and fiqh"}, {"id": "6a04e136-dd4e-4d3f-8a4e-2c258bfa556c", "type": "concept", "title": "Ummah and diaspora"}], "description": "Students examine Quran, Muhammad, hadith, law, Sunni/Shii traditions, Sufism, ethics, art, reform, and modern politics."}]}, {"id": "9ea81f50-b2cd-4cb3-9f87-44e52127ab38", "type": "topic", "title": "Religion, Ethics, Gender, Race, Caste, and Social Power", "description": "Religion can authorize hierarchy, sustain care, inspire critique, and support liberation. Students learn to analyze moral claims and social power together.", "children": [{"id": "f89bb2ec-b97e-462b-a3f1-4eceeaf37250", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Religious ethics", "children": [{"id": "d82f4de9-4eb1-4385-9a48-97d2a9e67a7a", "type": "concept", "title": "Virtue, law, command, compassion"}, {"id": "4c5b4d04-440b-413e-ab74-43f8d7c33300", "type": "concept", "title": "Case-based ethics"}, {"id": "0f12d990-8456-423b-9898-60d0e63a3540", "type": "concept", "title": "Pluralism and disagreement"}], "description": "Students compare sources of moral authority and how traditions reason about justice, violence, sexuality, medicine, poverty, and ecology."}, {"id": "ffc06cbf-0f1f-44ba-b8f9-f85a74ebb26a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Gender and sexuality", "children": [{"id": "d6279321-fe77-4bdc-a2b2-e6609bccea9d", "type": "concept", "title": "Women and leadership"}, {"id": "56d1e800-ee71-4dd8-85f4-e41c67a956f1", "type": "concept", "title": "Purity and family"}, {"id": "097bfa07-4416-4d26-94c4-d96d12c3dba3", "type": "concept", "title": "Queer religious life"}], "description": "The section examines bodies, authority, family, ritual roles, queer religion, feminist critique, and contested interpretation."}, {"id": "220e573e-b847-4b13-bb3e-edc76e6453c8", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Race, caste, and colonial power", "children": [{"id": "08c56e7c-a1da-427f-a2e8-ca677b07c68d", "type": "concept", "title": "Caste and ritual status"}, {"id": "dad380dc-a626-4e71-85e8-77ec40815739", "type": "concept", "title": "Race and theology"}, {"id": "841d88fe-6775-4ade-bddd-b96bb588ece5", "type": "concept", "title": "Liberation movements"}], "description": "Students analyze how religion interacts with racialization, caste, slavery, empire, nationalism, and anti-colonial resistance."}]}, {"id": "6010e226-1712-4d85-b9e1-21e1985b6d77", "type": "topic", "title": "Religion, Politics, Law, Nationalism, and Violence/Peacebuilding", "description": "This section equips students to discuss public religion carefully: neither romanticizing religion as peace nor reducing it to conflict.", "children": [{"id": "da4224be-cbca-48e1-9b4e-f910fd873ab4", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Religion and law", "children": [{"id": "8f7b6ca0-4656-4452-b063-93bd659930a4", "type": "concept", "title": "Religious freedom"}, {"id": "340dd69c-5e4f-41f8-bcb5-945c9b9c0a57", "type": "concept", "title": "Establishment and secularism"}, {"id": "da1d3070-5de8-4fc6-9174-3122773363e4", "type": "concept", "title": "Minority rights"}], "description": "Students learn how states define, protect, restrict, or regulate religion through constitutions, courts, policy, and human rights frameworks."}, {"id": "2a46229b-4af7-4934-9b11-9cc08059d379", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Nationalism and public religion", "children": [{"id": "1a95f4f0-56c6-4332-918f-e7ea4ec09a5f", "type": "concept", "title": "Civil religion"}, {"id": "64dce595-d8ae-473c-b343-b8495662d1e2", "type": "concept", "title": "Majoritarian politics"}, {"id": "5c8c408d-1a52-4389-989e-f82ce5b1e92c", "type": "concept", "title": "Sacred history and territory"}], "description": "Students study how religious symbols and histories can be mobilized for national identity, legitimacy, and exclusion."}, {"id": "4b5c3bf6-2e60-426e-b52a-449463f4e626", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Violence and peacebuilding", "children": [{"id": "13809656-44d8-4c69-ad0c-9b255685fb9a", "type": "concept", "title": "Martyrdom and militancy"}, {"id": "d5bb193a-4acb-45b8-93cc-d09875e6e9b5", "type": "concept", "title": "Nonviolence"}, {"id": "7a091671-4067-4364-87da-b0af9a159285", "type": "concept", "title": "Interfaith peacebuilding"}], "description": "The goal is to analyze when religion is a factor in conflict, reconciliation, nonviolence, humanitarian work, and transitional justice."}]}, {"id": "67250427-cc2a-4c15-a4fe-7bb782715f8b", "type": "topic", "title": "Secularization, Nonreligion, New Religious Movements, Media, and Globalization", "description": "Modernity does not simply erase religion. Students study secular identities, new movements, media forms, markets, migration, and global transformations.", "children": [{"id": "16c0430f-30f6-446e-952d-43b4fee2e10c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Secularization and nonreligion", "children": [{"id": "3fa684a3-821e-45e7-bb3a-6f58461e4b4a", "type": "concept", "title": "Secularism vs. secularization"}, {"id": "d4133616-73bc-4de3-8963-67948dca4783", "type": "concept", "title": "Atheism and humanism"}, {"id": "451aaf15-c8f6-461c-951f-e5b566dddaae", "type": "concept", "title": "Spiritual but not religious"}], "description": "Students compare theories of decline, privatization, differentiation, secular identity, atheism, spirituality, and the 'nones'."}, {"id": "dd838987-a077-4e1e-baec-55cc75c3614c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "New religious movements", "children": [{"id": "3333c050-cd52-441d-b2e1-b533758f57c0", "type": "concept", "title": "Charismatic authority"}, {"id": "540766e6-5918-4c6c-8307-ae0b67d00067", "type": "concept", "title": "Conversion and exit"}, {"id": "c72cd79a-8f97-42ad-a388-26cbc6bba246", "type": "concept", "title": "Public fear and labeling"}], "description": "Students learn to analyze emergence, conversion, charisma, controversy, and media narratives without sensationalism."}, {"id": "01dbcbac-2c94-4a35-be3a-a5286d931956", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Media and globalization", "children": [{"id": "b48dbccc-d7e3-4938-97bd-1b165ec631f7", "type": "concept", "title": "Digital religion"}, {"id": "620450b1-01c0-4a90-929b-da23d68f0c2b", "type": "concept", "title": "Celebrity and authority"}, {"id": "12dd1b42-7de1-43ae-a16d-cf4ee09b8f2f", "type": "concept", "title": "Global flows and local adaptation"}], "description": "The section examines how print, radio, television, apps, social media, migration, and transnational networks reshape religion."}]}, {"id": "358e827f-68e4-4271-81d2-10226312e31b", "type": "topic", "title": "Fieldwork, Public Religious Literacy, Research Ethics, and Capstone Analysis", "description": "The final section turns students into careful interpreters. They integrate methods, ethics, writing, and public explanation in a small research project.", "children": [{"id": "062531de-a409-457a-ac24-b3c939c8e1aa", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Research ethics and positionality", "children": [{"id": "bbbd25ac-f68d-4d1f-81cd-370ed24fab6c", "type": "concept", "title": "IRB basics"}, {"id": "a30660d5-02c9-4fd1-949e-3981c2ccde99", "type": "concept", "title": "Anonymity and consent"}, {"id": "a2fcc420-8291-4497-abb1-f1a1cdf3527e", "type": "concept", "title": "Reflexivity"}], "description": "Students learn consent, privacy, respectful observation, citation, and how their own location affects interpretation."}, {"id": "e4a8de55-e103-4ff6-a9cd-73ce50ffcdf2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Mini research portfolio", "children": [{"id": "f6f28529-35e3-47ad-8b64-6db183e89571", "type": "concept", "title": "Research question"}, {"id": "c814c3f6-130e-40fc-9c8d-d2910c8dc97e", "type": "concept", "title": "Evidence log"}, {"id": "f3440b75-cd61-4b7f-b9ee-33ec6fd4ade9", "type": "concept", "title": "Argument and revision"}], "description": "Students assemble a question, evidence set, method justification, annotated bibliography, analysis, and limitations."}, {"id": "5b0fd0af-7145-45a8-8482-6a0b9a5862ba", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Public religious literacy", "children": [{"id": "85695b0f-0f25-4c0a-ac7c-2e2d71670074", "type": "concept", "title": "Public-facing summary"}, {"id": "987a2193-e705-459a-807a-79ba74972c31", "type": "concept", "title": "Data and sources"}, {"id": "aec59c77-9a51-4660-b564-72db17572169", "type": "concept", "title": "Responsible disagreement"}], "description": "Students practice explaining religion clearly for non-specialists while preserving nuance and avoiding stereotype."}]}], "dbBacked": true}$irelsjson$::jsonb,
  true,
  false,
  'course',
  'public'
where not exists (
  select 1 from public.learning_paths where slug = 'introduction-to-religious-studies'
);

update public.learning_paths
set
  title = 'Introduction to Religious Studies',
  goal = 'Introduction to Religious Studies',
  summary = 'Introduction to Religious Studies introduces students to the academic study of religion as a complex human phenomenon expressed through texts, rituals, bodies, spaces, ethics, institutions, politics, and everyday life. Students learn how scholars define religion, compare traditions responsibly, interpret primary sources, analyze lived practice, and discuss religious diversity without reducing traditions to stereotypes or devotional claims.',
  data = $irelsjson${"slug": "introduction-to-religious-studies", "title": "Introduction to Religious Studies", "description": "Introduction to Religious Studies introduces students to the academic study of religion as a complex human phenomenon expressed through texts, rituals, bodies, spaces, ethics, institutions, politics, and everyday life. Students learn how scholars define religion, compare traditions responsibly, interpret primary sources, analyze lived practice, and discuss religious diversity without reducing traditions to stereotypes or devotional claims.", "topics": [{"id": "6ea152ad-9b82-4f18-9b90-23c3246e18e8", "type": "topic", "title": "Religion as an Academic Object of Study", "description": "This opening section establishes the course's non-devotional, non-sectarian frame. Students learn to study religion as a human, historical, social, textual, material, and experiential phenomenon while taking practitioners seriously.", "children": [{"id": "df2a9c1a-dcab-4b82-ad47-18af0dfc6d72", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Academic vs. confessional study", "children": [{"id": "9612ff0f-f57e-4ffd-a606-6b13791bf3f6", "type": "concept", "title": "Theology, apologetics, and religious studies"}, {"id": "52d569a5-5e1c-4b35-bb16-95d33c84691f", "type": "concept", "title": "Insider and outsider perspectives"}, {"id": "2fbef32e-054f-4b49-802c-1d014b9ba0c9", "type": "concept", "title": "Description before evaluation"}], "description": "Students learn the difference between studying religion from within a tradition and analyzing religion with public, evidence-based academic tools."}, {"id": "f5e1fe7e-a413-4430-87e5-1ea120f548c9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Religion in human life", "children": [{"id": "cfe4c778-76b6-4e42-a9e5-cae0e37205e1", "type": "concept", "title": "Belief and practice"}, {"id": "84dbc32d-abe0-4fe0-97e0-5a07fbf03268", "type": "concept", "title": "Identity and belonging"}, {"id": "7fa581bb-e983-4fda-8a34-56a1917317e2", "type": "concept", "title": "Public and private religion"}], "description": "The goal is to see religion as a force in meaning-making, identity, institutions, art, law, politics, conflict, and care."}, {"id": "adfa65a6-1228-496f-90ff-62353cc2644f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Religious literacy", "children": [{"id": "d872f951-663c-4bc1-a591-09e09c5dd961", "type": "concept", "title": "Basic vocabulary"}, {"id": "202eedbf-40ae-4075-a621-322cb7725815", "type": "concept", "title": "Avoiding essentialism"}, {"id": "79cb4fab-c598-42e7-943e-c7b54792b9c2", "type": "concept", "title": "Public consequences of religious ignorance"}], "description": "Students practice accurate, respectful, and critical explanation of traditions without stereotyping or flattening difference."}]}, {"id": "c2cdc9fa-73bd-48d8-a9df-4330da3560ec", "type": "topic", "title": "Defining Religion: The Sacred, Lived Religion, and Critical Categories", "description": "Definitions of religion are tools, not neutral facts. This section helps students understand why definitions matter, what they reveal, and what they obscure.", "children": [{"id": "1818a6e5-5fe5-485e-9a9a-b81245e4f515", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Substantive and functional definitions", "children": [{"id": "834f4eac-d579-4d2e-9920-a85bd1e02604", "type": "concept", "title": "Sacred/profane"}, {"id": "5046cd6a-9553-4bf5-87ab-f25df7e828cf", "type": "concept", "title": "Ultimate concern"}, {"id": "dcc5be8d-0511-44ff-b4e4-6094e7ef1a62", "type": "concept", "title": "Social function"}], "description": "Students compare definitions that focus on gods, sacred realities, ultimate concern, social cohesion, experience, or practice."}, {"id": "9cd07bd0-444d-4440-8df3-8bb26baed34c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Lived religion", "children": [{"id": "59075ddd-b664-423b-b169-fcbef9ae4291", "type": "concept", "title": "Everyday practice"}, {"id": "36964ca6-21b2-46e1-bc3e-46691380e67b", "type": "concept", "title": "Domestic religion"}, {"id": "de1a0c2d-8801-4e93-bc02-a2e783b7e651", "type": "concept", "title": "Popular religion"}], "description": "This approach shifts attention from official doctrine to what people actually do in homes, streets, bodies, calendars, and online spaces."}, {"id": "590f9e0d-4ee2-4f6b-817e-4530d5af2c29", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Category critique", "children": [{"id": "b97f615d-a958-4abd-8579-a7e80909a04d", "type": "concept", "title": "World religions model"}, {"id": "5f60d820-5613-4601-84be-620c408b83ce", "type": "concept", "title": "Race and empire"}, {"id": "12860c53-6889-4123-8e75-7aa3db3d333a", "type": "concept", "title": "Religion vs. culture"}], "description": "Students ask who invented categories, who benefits from them, and how colonial, legal, or academic classification shapes what counts as religion."}]}, {"id": "3c5f0270-5ced-423e-bfca-c97a98079426", "type": "topic", "title": "Methods and Evidence: Text, History, Ethnography, Material Culture, and Digital Sources", "description": "An introductory course teaches students how knowledge is made. This section introduces the major evidence types and methods used by scholars of religion.", "children": [{"id": "0b2004a7-5083-47d0-90d6-2be10fd922f5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Textual and historical methods", "children": [{"id": "f03eeb00-7d0e-4489-9a3a-a990b46ea89b", "type": "concept", "title": "Authorship and audience"}, {"id": "2666c3e2-2a32-4e21-887a-a93ce20c1c7c", "type": "concept", "title": "Canon and commentary"}, {"id": "29778195-7185-46c6-9105-1066f5d037a8", "type": "concept", "title": "Historical-critical questions"}], "description": "Students learn to read sources in context, identify genre, track transmission, and distinguish primary from secondary evidence."}, {"id": "a030d526-1773-4848-88d4-2f986060292b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ethnography and field observation", "children": [{"id": "7a47bdfd-cbc1-4585-ad37-4a6bc072587d", "type": "concept", "title": "Participant observation"}, {"id": "0d8c691c-27de-4239-ab3c-050163179aa4", "type": "concept", "title": "Interview ethics"}, {"id": "8c7d4176-83e6-4c81-86c9-98a4acfb6cd9", "type": "concept", "title": "Thick description"}], "description": "Students learn how interviews, observation, fieldnotes, and reflexivity help scholars study living communities responsibly."}, {"id": "789424ab-e2f4-45e7-9c1a-fd180cc2cd2e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Material and digital religion", "children": [{"id": "9c6959aa-9560-4c4e-85ff-3f969811846c", "type": "concept", "title": "Sacred space"}, {"id": "75447d4c-2841-4212-b85c-6bb070335a7d", "type": "concept", "title": "Digital rituals"}, {"id": "f3471345-b1f2-4880-abfd-edd45379d58c", "type": "concept", "title": "Archives and metadata"}], "description": "Students treat objects, architecture, sound, images, apps, and online communities as evidence rather than decoration."}]}, {"id": "bffd6340-832c-4d82-9d5a-c1f0a61714a6", "type": "topic", "title": "Myth, Narrative, Scripture, and Interpretation", "description": "Stories and texts do religious work: they organize memory, authority, ethics, identity, and time. This section trains students to interpret them carefully.", "children": [{"id": "1fb86d70-a485-4aea-b91b-a9b6a0357479", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Myth and sacred narrative", "children": [{"id": "bf51d643-cff3-425f-842f-918bf0eb1972", "type": "concept", "title": "Origins and cosmogony"}, {"id": "dc0a52b9-ee39-4e9c-b17c-fecce9885ed1", "type": "concept", "title": "Heroes and ancestors"}, {"id": "4c26a4c8-1fe5-42d5-be16-ba8ecc983259", "type": "concept", "title": "Ritual retelling"}], "description": "Students learn that myth is not simply falsehood; in religious studies it often names meaningful narrative that structures a world."}, {"id": "1e9c949e-ccf8-4934-ba83-5b46763c1b9d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Scripture and canon", "children": [{"id": "39a67341-b538-4d21-a50f-0bef4b1a584b", "type": "concept", "title": "Canon formation"}, {"id": "908eb686-b0a8-41ed-ac10-3fa6361c682c", "type": "concept", "title": "Translation"}, {"id": "3d7f43ef-a829-4993-9f52-5809a4729b2f", "type": "concept", "title": "Commentary traditions"}], "description": "Students explore how communities authorize texts, preserve them, translate them, and debate their meanings."}, {"id": "157fb6a0-5f6e-4146-ae98-929572b8a0fc", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Hermeneutics", "children": [{"id": "ff231fc8-bfa5-4c12-b18d-2569492ad5c1", "type": "concept", "title": "Reader and community"}, {"id": "2e529813-c660-4515-90fc-2504cfd3bc34", "type": "concept", "title": "Context and reception"}, {"id": "f08d8e79-eaa8-447e-8db2-ac356a9c4a03", "type": "concept", "title": "Interpretive conflict"}], "description": "Students compare interpretive strategies across literal, allegorical, legal, mystical, historical, and liberationist readings."}]}, {"id": "717cc052-0ce9-4598-8e51-8064505e2fdf", "type": "topic", "title": "Ritual, Practice, Embodiment, and Religious Experience", "description": "Religion is not only believed; it is enacted. This section helps students analyze repeated practices, disciplined bodies, emotion, memory, and experience.", "children": [{"id": "f3dc693f-5efd-4cca-b683-affe5c852d15", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ritual form and function", "children": [{"id": "fad2cade-8345-4314-85c9-a5623b8a7ef3", "type": "concept", "title": "Rites of passage"}, {"id": "68d36b62-5e26-4be0-9759-30024bc9316f", "type": "concept", "title": "Calendar rituals"}, {"id": "b7b3777d-ab2a-462d-afcd-d76c53add652", "type": "concept", "title": "Purity and sacrifice"}], "description": "Students learn to identify ritual sequences, roles, objects, spaces, and social effects."}, {"id": "50c2ab8b-ae2f-469c-9a9a-ac8679d43d00", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Embodiment and discipline", "children": [{"id": "69f85165-4666-48a2-8128-d73135d7bf8a", "type": "concept", "title": "Asceticism"}, {"id": "46693568-407e-4a1d-a47c-d8b4f33b99d1", "type": "concept", "title": "Sensory practice"}, {"id": "3e2f5a51-c175-4ba2-83eb-c9fb1297109d", "type": "concept", "title": "Gendered bodies"}], "description": "The section highlights bodies in prayer, fasting, meditation, pilgrimage, dress, food, healing, and performance."}, {"id": "8f50b07d-a426-4648-a975-f9d7068333e2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Religious experience", "children": [{"id": "2952f7a3-a6cb-4f3a-97bf-084887e8745d", "type": "concept", "title": "Mysticism"}, {"id": "12f85307-3121-4ff5-b703-531cf7cf0af9", "type": "concept", "title": "Conversion narratives"}, {"id": "c1d03d81-c43d-42e6-b7d1-207296c47f51", "type": "concept", "title": "Psychology and culture"}], "description": "Students consider visions, conversion, mystical states, possession, awe, grief, and healing without reducing them to one explanation."}]}, {"id": "ff783fdb-9241-4fd6-b0c6-7d02315bb183", "type": "topic", "title": "Symbol, Image, Sound, Space, and Material Culture", "description": "Material culture shows how religion becomes visible, audible, tangible, and contested. Students learn to analyze things and spaces as arguments about value and authority.", "children": [{"id": "1835dafa-baee-4205-b22d-a64af4f8113a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Symbols and images", "children": [{"id": "9e12e7ba-0df4-4c37-bd5e-e6d602bad06f", "type": "concept", "title": "Icon and iconoclasm"}, {"id": "1a7d977b-e689-49c4-97da-5b3a2f1f8fb1", "type": "concept", "title": "Dress and identity"}, {"id": "33ceba4a-2b0b-4df7-942a-aca1a1e76dbf", "type": "concept", "title": "Art and devotion"}], "description": "Students ask how signs, icons, clothing, colors, and visual forms condense complex meanings."}, {"id": "26e3e1a6-6732-4d50-ad49-d0171dd51922", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Sacred space", "children": [{"id": "5d5ba2bf-297a-4650-a90c-88b85ef57d32", "type": "concept", "title": "Architecture"}, {"id": "bdbb82d8-a372-40c9-9344-c6503228f0d8", "type": "concept", "title": "Pilgrimage sites"}, {"id": "b0e5c620-33e9-4973-8879-8e361ce51657", "type": "concept", "title": "Place-making"}], "description": "Students analyze temples, churches, mosques, synagogues, shrines, homes, landscapes, memorials, and digital spaces."}, {"id": "6c917a16-cc58-4b8a-808c-500f4f27d0a0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Sound, food, and objects", "children": [{"id": "f0feac86-5ecb-41a9-a769-decb90dfd8f7", "type": "concept", "title": "Music and chant"}, {"id": "54fd6bf8-e2e5-492d-ba64-cb775747be92", "type": "concept", "title": "Food laws"}, {"id": "53c887b8-9f51-4b16-802b-a7194a65e4fb", "type": "concept", "title": "Relics and commodities"}], "description": "The goal is to notice how music, chant, bells, foodways, relics, tools, and commodities carry religious meaning."}]}, {"id": "fadfc15e-b9a7-4c7c-b90a-ebf033e8c4de", "type": "topic", "title": "Community, Authority, Institutions, and Tradition-Making", "description": "Traditions are maintained and changed by communities. This section studies leadership, law, lineage, institutions, memory, reform, and conflict.", "children": [{"id": "05034e21-eae1-427c-8ac2-cafadd9ef564", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Authority and leadership", "children": [{"id": "b839de5c-c934-440b-a18f-71191daa767c", "type": "concept", "title": "Priests, rabbis, imams, monks, gurus"}, {"id": "8c02123b-5b23-4156-9bb4-e4809b19f493", "type": "concept", "title": "Charisma and office"}, {"id": "a439a876-e349-4ba1-a82f-fce2b67797bb", "type": "concept", "title": "Gender and authority"}], "description": "Students examine how authority is justified through charisma, office, scripture, lineage, expertise, revelation, or community recognition."}, {"id": "4b9c39d0-6a12-4fb6-a39a-34d79db9d23c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Institutions and law", "children": [{"id": "065cc13f-d312-4e57-aaed-091fd1f0aefd", "type": "concept", "title": "Congregations and orders"}, {"id": "acffe68e-d7e5-4c47-97b5-2d69ea51b2c5", "type": "concept", "title": "Religious law"}, {"id": "b0ee5291-4a91-4b5f-822d-862086c8fdc4", "type": "concept", "title": "Institutional accountability"}], "description": "Students learn how religious organizations structure education, charity, discipline, courts, finance, and public influence."}, {"id": "cd68e5c9-f0b0-48d5-bb7f-4ece4cea3eb2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Continuity and change", "children": [{"id": "6a8f99c1-81cb-4711-a347-f2c00ceaf0cb", "type": "concept", "title": "Reform movements"}, {"id": "0217a951-7038-4f57-8462-d051e8f835a4", "type": "concept", "title": "Sect and denomination"}, {"id": "b2635ebb-cc7e-4d8a-9a4a-cc09d469d5a3", "type": "concept", "title": "Memory and invention"}], "description": "The section frames reform, revival, schism, syncretism, and innovation as normal parts of religious history."}]}, {"id": "cc3bd060-669d-43ea-b856-58a1ef52f2d2", "type": "topic", "title": "Comparison, Translation, Classification, and World Religions", "description": "Comparison is powerful but risky. Students learn to compare responsibly, recognize the limits of the world religions model, and avoid false equivalence.", "children": [{"id": "a06a9bdf-c7cc-43e9-915e-0056397d3e8f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Why compare?", "children": [{"id": "f79db83c-364c-4e9e-a1e8-dadc6639fc77", "type": "concept", "title": "Controlled comparison"}, {"id": "0a5bd0a2-9be6-4bad-ae45-ea5261043e9b", "type": "concept", "title": "Difference and similarity"}, {"id": "f879deac-ea06-42e2-9612-044fb6a44b83", "type": "concept", "title": "Scale of analysis"}], "description": "Students learn how comparison can clarify patterns, differences, questions, and assumptions when done with precision."}, {"id": "76209bdf-4688-41ba-9cef-eea257091a9f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Translation problems", "children": [{"id": "d5294f87-f7a6-4fe8-8414-a61e0b89f5fa", "type": "concept", "title": "Untranslatable terms"}, {"id": "38a9bd38-110d-48be-8171-956f5c7a07b3", "type": "concept", "title": "Colonial translation"}, {"id": "f87acc64-6f7d-4daf-9286-342b0a2fd21c", "type": "concept", "title": "Conceptual humility"}], "description": "Students explore why terms such as religion, scripture, god, ritual, caste, dharma, and secular do not map cleanly across languages."}, {"id": "38931215-39d8-463b-bdcc-9349cbbb68af", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Classification and power", "children": [{"id": "dbe5febf-d2c3-4e65-8efc-4c527dcac80b", "type": "concept", "title": "World religions textbooks"}, {"id": "862ff9d0-d566-4cf6-995b-c8e0f267a068", "type": "concept", "title": "Legal definitions"}, {"id": "a8afd787-4b44-4d99-bf1f-5f2b3c12d87d", "type": "concept", "title": "Minority traditions"}], "description": "The section asks how museum labels, census categories, law, textbooks, and media shape public understanding."}]}, {"id": "105b29dd-293f-408b-8001-c1abd045bf2d", "type": "topic", "title": "Indigenous, Diasporic, and Local Traditions", "description": "This section resists treating religion as only formal doctrine or large world traditions. It centers place, land, memory, kinship, diaspora, and colonial history.", "children": [{"id": "e397c500-9e30-4ea3-a1b6-9ce4d408e646", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Indigenous traditions and land", "children": [{"id": "d864d6aa-b3ba-4640-b488-7fd6316ab4f1", "type": "concept", "title": "Oral tradition"}, {"id": "6de7d96b-159f-48cf-b394-e2cbe1c10a1c", "type": "concept", "title": "Sacred landscapes"}, {"id": "8f34bb33-e597-45d2-8afe-763665ff2d09", "type": "concept", "title": "Colonial disruption"}], "description": "Students learn why land, ancestors, ceremony, ecology, and sovereignty are often inseparable."}, {"id": "be50cc96-c736-4e84-b43e-defec740179b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Diaspora and migration", "children": [{"id": "e95d49f9-dd32-4e86-944e-ba2d94a81739", "type": "concept", "title": "Temples and community centers"}, {"id": "03c83ded-4da5-44af-af0d-e66d0b3dd634", "type": "concept", "title": "Hybridity"}, {"id": "5125c868-9f29-450a-be5c-83c35b703ad7", "type": "concept", "title": "Generational change"}], "description": "Students study how religious communities adapt across borders while preserving memory, ritual, and identity."}, {"id": "2b50ac68-c576-48e9-984d-54570e654300", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Local religion", "children": [{"id": "38693969-cc62-4e2c-870f-878b99f85ab3", "type": "concept", "title": "Household shrines"}, {"id": "1a171277-9004-45dd-9c91-cba9dcbe28f9", "type": "concept", "title": "Festivals"}, {"id": "c8c79b6f-252c-4760-8947-14014606a7fa", "type": "concept", "title": "Neighborhood religious economies"}], "description": "The goal is to see local practice as theoretically important, not a deviation from official tradition."}]}, {"id": "c0a1ead9-ef46-4e20-a5fd-433e33a8e0a5", "type": "topic", "title": "Asian Religious Traditions in Context", "description": "This section introduces major Asian traditions through history, practice, texts, philosophy, and lived communities while avoiding one-note summaries.", "children": [{"id": "c8f840f2-87c1-4aed-b6f9-996936a23651", "type": "subtopic", "title": "South Asian traditions", "children": [{"id": "2ab69dad-f0d7-4286-b970-a78189140903", "type": "concept", "title": "Vedas and Upanishads"}, {"id": "67e10794-f98d-4809-91bc-216a88e5daec", "type": "concept", "title": "Bhakti and puja"}, {"id": "8497ffb8-b5af-4e6b-9aa0-c1b3a597515c", "type": "concept", "title": "Karma, dharma, moksha"}], "description": "Students encounter Hindu, Buddhist, Jain, and Sikh traditions through ritual, philosophy, devotion, caste, renunciation, and modernity."}, {"id": "e607cd11-aea6-44d4-acee-0851bd675727", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Buddhist traditions", "children": [{"id": "9b3fc021-085c-48fb-8e02-b8e0ed375209", "type": "concept", "title": "Four Noble Truths"}, {"id": "c5ee17a1-a1f3-4cc6-9094-98baa292da37", "type": "concept", "title": "Sangha"}, {"id": "fb0e335c-4da1-43eb-9c6d-a9915889a183", "type": "concept", "title": "Bodhisattva ideals"}], "description": "Students trace Buddhism from early teachings to Theravada, Mahayana, Vajrayana, monasticism, meditation, and global modern forms."}, {"id": "48daa5fa-cbcc-40ce-84b5-6001c225809e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "East Asian traditions", "children": [{"id": "dafba335-a27c-49eb-be18-790f38c3d6bd", "type": "concept", "title": "Ancestor rites"}, {"id": "18f311fa-8321-49f0-9413-20ab2ac71280", "type": "concept", "title": "Dao and ritual"}, {"id": "197d5c1c-0d0f-4d37-882c-3b49511a5921", "type": "concept", "title": "Kami and shrine practice"}], "description": "Students examine Confucian, Daoist, Buddhist, Shinto, and popular traditions in relation to family, state, ritual, and cosmology."}]}, {"id": "03e77945-c930-46cd-be0d-88d884e2de3b", "type": "topic", "title": "Abrahamic Traditions in Context", "description": "Students study Judaism, Christianity, and Islam historically and comparatively without treating them as identical branches of a single template.", "children": [{"id": "a5a36669-1c1f-4858-9e4e-8be8e7ccd418", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Judaism", "children": [{"id": "950d7a66-d2d5-4cbd-916d-78f5de6206e4", "type": "concept", "title": "Tanakh and Talmud"}, {"id": "f2e3a722-0370-4dd7-968b-c17e2d6d7975", "type": "concept", "title": "Halakhah"}, {"id": "94e1033d-44ca-44d8-9d73-1fb51249175f", "type": "concept", "title": "Diaspora and modern Jewish life"}], "description": "Students explore Torah, rabbinic tradition, law, ritual calendar, diaspora, memory, modernity, and diverse Jewish identities."}, {"id": "1f0d2a16-74a2-4c22-83f8-49653a17d003", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Christianity", "children": [{"id": "59a04f42-b854-4467-a7c3-ffb5f86221dc", "type": "concept", "title": "New Testament contexts"}, {"id": "81868e74-fd86-440d-b4f3-59ae54817e72", "type": "concept", "title": "Creeds and councils"}, {"id": "d4e640a9-ac69-4da9-87cf-3f7614105291", "type": "concept", "title": "Reformation and global Christianity"}], "description": "Students study Jesus movements, church formation, doctrine, sacraments, reform, missions, colonialism, and global Christianity."}, {"id": "ecbb325d-a057-40d4-980b-a38c7d21005d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Islam", "children": [{"id": "e1464c44-320d-494d-9455-09e2b474144f", "type": "concept", "title": "Quran and hadith"}, {"id": "0914add5-c1aa-4784-95b9-d9137d26a5c1", "type": "concept", "title": "Sharia and fiqh"}, {"id": "6a04e136-dd4e-4d3f-8a4e-2c258bfa556c", "type": "concept", "title": "Ummah and diaspora"}], "description": "Students examine Quran, Muhammad, hadith, law, Sunni/Shii traditions, Sufism, ethics, art, reform, and modern politics."}]}, {"id": "9ea81f50-b2cd-4cb3-9f87-44e52127ab38", "type": "topic", "title": "Religion, Ethics, Gender, Race, Caste, and Social Power", "description": "Religion can authorize hierarchy, sustain care, inspire critique, and support liberation. Students learn to analyze moral claims and social power together.", "children": [{"id": "f89bb2ec-b97e-462b-a3f1-4eceeaf37250", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Religious ethics", "children": [{"id": "d82f4de9-4eb1-4385-9a48-97d2a9e67a7a", "type": "concept", "title": "Virtue, law, command, compassion"}, {"id": "4c5b4d04-440b-413e-ab74-43f8d7c33300", "type": "concept", "title": "Case-based ethics"}, {"id": "0f12d990-8456-423b-9898-60d0e63a3540", "type": "concept", "title": "Pluralism and disagreement"}], "description": "Students compare sources of moral authority and how traditions reason about justice, violence, sexuality, medicine, poverty, and ecology."}, {"id": "ffc06cbf-0f1f-44ba-b8f9-f85a74ebb26a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Gender and sexuality", "children": [{"id": "d6279321-fe77-4bdc-a2b2-e6609bccea9d", "type": "concept", "title": "Women and leadership"}, {"id": "56d1e800-ee71-4dd8-85f4-e41c67a956f1", "type": "concept", "title": "Purity and family"}, {"id": "097bfa07-4416-4d26-94c4-d96d12c3dba3", "type": "concept", "title": "Queer religious life"}], "description": "The section examines bodies, authority, family, ritual roles, queer religion, feminist critique, and contested interpretation."}, {"id": "220e573e-b847-4b13-bb3e-edc76e6453c8", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Race, caste, and colonial power", "children": [{"id": "08c56e7c-a1da-427f-a2e8-ca677b07c68d", "type": "concept", "title": "Caste and ritual status"}, {"id": "dad380dc-a626-4e71-85e8-77ec40815739", "type": "concept", "title": "Race and theology"}, {"id": "841d88fe-6775-4ade-bddd-b96bb588ece5", "type": "concept", "title": "Liberation movements"}], "description": "Students analyze how religion interacts with racialization, caste, slavery, empire, nationalism, and anti-colonial resistance."}]}, {"id": "6010e226-1712-4d85-b9e1-21e1985b6d77", "type": "topic", "title": "Religion, Politics, Law, Nationalism, and Violence/Peacebuilding", "description": "This section equips students to discuss public religion carefully: neither romanticizing religion as peace nor reducing it to conflict.", "children": [{"id": "da4224be-cbca-48e1-9b4e-f910fd873ab4", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Religion and law", "children": [{"id": "8f7b6ca0-4656-4452-b063-93bd659930a4", "type": "concept", "title": "Religious freedom"}, {"id": "340dd69c-5e4f-41f8-bcb5-945c9b9c0a57", "type": "concept", "title": "Establishment and secularism"}, {"id": "da1d3070-5de8-4fc6-9174-3122773363e4", "type": "concept", "title": "Minority rights"}], "description": "Students learn how states define, protect, restrict, or regulate religion through constitutions, courts, policy, and human rights frameworks."}, {"id": "2a46229b-4af7-4934-9b11-9cc08059d379", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Nationalism and public religion", "children": [{"id": "1a95f4f0-56c6-4332-918f-e7ea4ec09a5f", "type": "concept", "title": "Civil religion"}, {"id": "64dce595-d8ae-473c-b343-b8495662d1e2", "type": "concept", "title": "Majoritarian politics"}, {"id": "5c8c408d-1a52-4389-989e-f82ce5b1e92c", "type": "concept", "title": "Sacred history and territory"}], "description": "Students study how religious symbols and histories can be mobilized for national identity, legitimacy, and exclusion."}, {"id": "4b5c3bf6-2e60-426e-b52a-449463f4e626", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Violence and peacebuilding", "children": [{"id": "13809656-44d8-4c69-ad0c-9b255685fb9a", "type": "concept", "title": "Martyrdom and militancy"}, {"id": "d5bb193a-4acb-45b8-93cc-d09875e6e9b5", "type": "concept", "title": "Nonviolence"}, {"id": "7a091671-4067-4364-87da-b0af9a159285", "type": "concept", "title": "Interfaith peacebuilding"}], "description": "The goal is to analyze when religion is a factor in conflict, reconciliation, nonviolence, humanitarian work, and transitional justice."}]}, {"id": "67250427-cc2a-4c15-a4fe-7bb782715f8b", "type": "topic", "title": "Secularization, Nonreligion, New Religious Movements, Media, and Globalization", "description": "Modernity does not simply erase religion. Students study secular identities, new movements, media forms, markets, migration, and global transformations.", "children": [{"id": "16c0430f-30f6-446e-952d-43b4fee2e10c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Secularization and nonreligion", "children": [{"id": "3fa684a3-821e-45e7-bb3a-6f58461e4b4a", "type": "concept", "title": "Secularism vs. secularization"}, {"id": "d4133616-73bc-4de3-8963-67948dca4783", "type": "concept", "title": "Atheism and humanism"}, {"id": "451aaf15-c8f6-461c-951f-e5b566dddaae", "type": "concept", "title": "Spiritual but not religious"}], "description": "Students compare theories of decline, privatization, differentiation, secular identity, atheism, spirituality, and the 'nones'."}, {"id": "dd838987-a077-4e1e-baec-55cc75c3614c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "New religious movements", "children": [{"id": "3333c050-cd52-441d-b2e1-b533758f57c0", "type": "concept", "title": "Charismatic authority"}, {"id": "540766e6-5918-4c6c-8307-ae0b67d00067", "type": "concept", "title": "Conversion and exit"}, {"id": "c72cd79a-8f97-42ad-a388-26cbc6bba246", "type": "concept", "title": "Public fear and labeling"}], "description": "Students learn to analyze emergence, conversion, charisma, controversy, and media narratives without sensationalism."}, {"id": "01dbcbac-2c94-4a35-be3a-a5286d931956", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Media and globalization", "children": [{"id": "b48dbccc-d7e3-4938-97bd-1b165ec631f7", "type": "concept", "title": "Digital religion"}, {"id": "620450b1-01c0-4a90-929b-da23d68f0c2b", "type": "concept", "title": "Celebrity and authority"}, {"id": "12dd1b42-7de1-43ae-a16d-cf4ee09b8f2f", "type": "concept", "title": "Global flows and local adaptation"}], "description": "The section examines how print, radio, television, apps, social media, migration, and transnational networks reshape religion."}]}, {"id": "358e827f-68e4-4271-81d2-10226312e31b", "type": "topic", "title": "Fieldwork, Public Religious Literacy, Research Ethics, and Capstone Analysis", "description": "The final section turns students into careful interpreters. They integrate methods, ethics, writing, and public explanation in a small research project.", "children": [{"id": "062531de-a409-457a-ac24-b3c939c8e1aa", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Research ethics and positionality", "children": [{"id": "bbbd25ac-f68d-4d1f-81cd-370ed24fab6c", "type": "concept", "title": "IRB basics"}, {"id": "a30660d5-02c9-4fd1-949e-3981c2ccde99", "type": "concept", "title": "Anonymity and consent"}, {"id": "a2fcc420-8291-4497-abb1-f1a1cdf3527e", "type": "concept", "title": "Reflexivity"}], "description": "Students learn consent, privacy, respectful observation, citation, and how their own location affects interpretation."}, {"id": "e4a8de55-e103-4ff6-a9cd-73ce50ffcdf2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Mini research portfolio", "children": [{"id": "f6f28529-35e3-47ad-8b64-6db183e89571", "type": "concept", "title": "Research question"}, {"id": "c814c3f6-130e-40fc-9c8d-d2910c8dc97e", "type": "concept", "title": "Evidence log"}, {"id": "f3440b75-cd61-4b7f-b9ee-33ec6fd4ade9", "type": "concept", "title": "Argument and revision"}], "description": "Students assemble a question, evidence set, method justification, annotated bibliography, analysis, and limitations."}, {"id": "5b0fd0af-7145-45a8-8482-6a0b9a5862ba", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Public religious literacy", "children": [{"id": "85695b0f-0f25-4c0a-ac7c-2e2d71670074", "type": "concept", "title": "Public-facing summary"}, {"id": "987a2193-e705-459a-807a-79ba74972c31", "type": "concept", "title": "Data and sources"}, {"id": "aec59c77-9a51-4660-b564-72db17572169", "type": "concept", "title": "Responsible disagreement"}], "description": "Students practice explaining religion clearly for non-specialists while preserving nuance and avoiding stereotype."}]}], "dbBacked": true}$irelsjson$::jsonb,
